<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kirana gharana</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kirana gharana is one of the main stylistic schools of Hindustani classical singing, named after the town of Kirana in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uttar-pradesh">Uttar Pradesh</a>. It was shaped in the late 19th and early 20th centuries by Abdul <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karim-khan-zand">Karim Khan</a>, who settled at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miraj">Miraj</a>, and Abdul Wahid Khan, and is known for slow, unfolding development of the raga and attention to exact pitch. Its later singers include Sawai Gandharva, Gangubai Hangal and Bhimsen Joshi.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kirana-gharana.jpg" alt="Kirana gharana" width="320" height="222" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ustad Mashkoor Ali Khan · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:KiranaGharanaFamilyTree.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
